WEBVTT

00:01.920 --> 00:06.640
Most ChatDBT users just open up the app or the websites and start typing.

00:07.680 --> 00:13.320
And that's definitely fine for low stakes work, but you're leaving a lot of performance on the table.

00:13.900 --> 00:18.720
Because ChatDBT actually gives eligible users access to much more powerful models,

00:18.880 --> 00:22.200
we call them reasoning models or thinking models or advanced models,

00:22.640 --> 00:26.260
and those can significantly improve the quality of the output.

00:26.260 --> 00:30.160
So if you look at the whole lineup of all of the ChatDBT models,

00:30.540 --> 00:34.600
it's really built around different levels of speed and reasoning.

00:35.500 --> 00:42.320
So the model that most questions get routed to is GPT 5.5 Instant,

00:42.860 --> 00:46.520
and this is actually a really good fast everyday model.

00:47.060 --> 00:52.420
It's designed for quick questions, for writing, summarizing, brainstorming, translation,

00:52.420 --> 00:56.820
even image analysis, and even explaining some concepts.

00:57.760 --> 01:03.340
But for higher stakes and more demanding work, this is where you want to get into these advanced models.

01:04.260 --> 01:11.400
So first of all, we have GPT 5.6 Sol, and you can use those through the thinking and pro options.

01:12.120 --> 01:17.720
And 5.6 Sol was released just last week, and this is OpenAI's flagship reasoning model

01:17.720 --> 01:22.420
designed to handle complex work from the beginning all the way until the end.

01:23.160 --> 01:28.700
So the biggest improvement here compared to the previous models is not that it's giving smarter answers,

01:28.700 --> 01:36.220
but it's also better at staying focused across longer workflows and using tools and checking results midway through,

01:36.760 --> 01:40.640
and then turning those rough inputs into more finished, ready to use outputs.

01:41.640 --> 01:48.120
The benchmarks are showing that this model is particularly strong in coding, in research, in presentations,

01:48.640 --> 01:54.780
documents, spreadsheets, design, science, cybersecurity, and other advanced professional work.

01:55.800 --> 01:58.120
There's a couple of other things that it's good at as well.

01:58.320 --> 02:03.960
It has a lot stronger visual judgment than the previous models, and computer use capabilities,

02:03.960 --> 02:06.360
meaning it can control your computer for you.

02:06.360 --> 02:12.260
So that means that it can inspect what it creates, identify problems on its own,

02:12.640 --> 02:16.500
and then refine the result rather than just simply handing you a first draft.

02:17.400 --> 02:21.640
And then it's also a lot more efficient, which is becoming increasingly important.

02:22.340 --> 02:27.140
OpenAI says that it can complete demanding tasks with fewer tokens, fewer steps,

02:27.360 --> 02:29.680
and with less guidance than earlier models.

02:31.160 --> 02:36.460
Now, the broader GPT 5.6 family also includes Terra and Luna,

02:36.800 --> 02:40.840
but these models are not available in a standard ChatTBT configuration.

02:41.940 --> 02:44.200
Okay, so how do we actually access these models?

02:44.340 --> 02:46.240
Well, first of all, we're going to open up a new chat,

02:46.940 --> 02:53.060
and then on the web version of ChatTBT, you'll find the selected models inside the model picker on mobile.

02:53.760 --> 02:56.540
The model name typically shows up on top of the screen.

02:57.280 --> 03:02.400
So you want to open up that model picker and choose the option that best fits your task.

03:02.980 --> 03:06.700
For most routine and conversational work, instant is fine,

03:07.100 --> 03:12.680
but whenever a task requires judgment, research, analysis, or several connected steps,

03:13.100 --> 03:15.540
then I would always manually select thinking.

03:16.400 --> 03:21.660
And for the most difficult or high stakes work where depth and accuracy matter more than speed,

03:21.860 --> 03:23.860
I always use Pro when it is available.

03:24.860 --> 03:28.860
In the GPT app, there are sometimes also other options available,

03:29.460 --> 03:34.760
like increasing the reasoning effort to medium, high, and extra high.

03:35.620 --> 03:40.540
Now, these deeper reasoning options usually take longer and they may use your credits more quickly,

03:40.920 --> 03:45.580
so you want to really save them for the work that genuinely benefits from the additional depth.

03:46.260 --> 03:51.100
One cool thing that has been added recently is that you can actually give more follow-up instructions

03:51.100 --> 03:53.800
or things that come to mind while it's processing,

03:54.320 --> 03:58.620
and then it will be included in its answer while it's creating the output.

03:59.140 --> 04:03.940
Another tip here is that if you realize after submitting your question or task

04:03.940 --> 04:08.840
that you really only needed a quick response, you can ask it to answer immediately,

04:09.300 --> 04:14.280
and you can also retry a completed response later using another available model.

04:15.080 --> 04:22.860
Now, in terms of the availability, the GPT 5.5 Instant remains the default everyday model across GPT plans,

04:23.260 --> 04:28.960
so you have to switch manually to other models like GPT 5.6 reasoning,

04:29.220 --> 04:33.460
for which you will need a plus plan while on Pro, business, and enterprise,

04:33.680 --> 04:37.560
you typically get more reasoning capacity and access to Pro.

04:38.360 --> 04:45.940
As of now, free, go, and users who haven't logged in to chat to GPT are not included in the 5.6 rollout.

04:46.920 --> 04:51.480
Now, all these exact options really depend on your plan, on your workspace settings,

04:51.620 --> 04:54.660
and which version of the model picker has been rolled out to you,

04:55.060 --> 04:58.040
so what you see may vary a bit from what we showed you today,

04:58.460 --> 05:03.220
but generally, choosing these advanced models really gives you a lot more reasoning power

05:03.220 --> 05:06.200
and therefore really increases the quality of your work.

